---
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import DenoiseHeading from "../components/motion/DenoiseHeading";

const sections = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "generative-modeling", label: "Generative Modeling" },
  { id: "structure-aware-sciml", label: "Structure-Aware SciML" },
  { id: "optimization-design", label: "Optimization & Design" },
  { id: "contact", label: "Collaboration/ Supervision" },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Research" sections={sections}>
  <section class="page-section research-overview" id="overview">
    <DenoiseHeading text="Research" as="h1" client:visible />
    <p class="section-eyebrow">Research vision</p>
    <p class="lead">
      My research lies at the intersection of scientific machine learning, generative modeling, and optimization.
      I am interested in developing methods that combine the flexibility of data-driven learning with the mathematical and physical structure of scientific problems.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      My long-term vision is to move beyond modeling physical systems towards automated scientific discovery and engineering design.
      By combining learned physical models, generative methods, and adaptive optimization, I want to develop systems that can explore complex design spaces and iteratively improve solutions through simulations and experimental feedback.
    </p>

    <p class="section-eyebrow">Main themes</p>
    <div class="theme-grid" aria-label="Research themes">
      <a class="theme-link-card" href="#generative-modeling">
        <h2>Generative Modeling</h2>
        <p>Flow matching and diffusion models for physical systems, constraints, and inverse problems.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="theme-link-card" href="#structure-aware-sciml">
        <h2>Structure-Aware SciML</h2>
        <p>Physics-informed learning, PDEs, operator learning, and surrogate modeling.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="theme-link-card" href="#optimization-design">
        <h2>Optimization & Design</h2>
        <p>Control, closed-loop optimization, and automated engineering design.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section research-theme" id="generative-modeling">
    <div class="theme-card">
      <p class="section-eyebrow theme-number">Theme 01</p>
      <DenoiseHeading text="Generative Modeling for Physical Systems" client:visible />
      <p>
        Generative models offer a flexible way to represent distributions over complex objects, including physical fields, trajectories, and solutions to partial differential equations. Unlike deterministic prediction, they can capture multiple plausible outcomes and provide a natural foundation for inference under incomplete information.
      </p>
      <p>
        My research investigates how generative models can incorporate physical constraints, learned geometric structure, and prior knowledge. This includes physics-constrained fine-tuning of flow-matching models for generation and inverse problems, as well as learning continuous-time generative dynamics that follow the geometry of observed data.
      </p>
      <p>
        A central question is how to combine the expressivity of learned distributions with the structure and constraints of scientific problems, enabling physically meaningful generation without unnecessarily restricting the range of plausible solutions.
      </p>
      <div class="keyword-row" aria-label="Key ideas for generative modeling">
        <span class="keyword-pill">Flow Matching</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Diffusion Models</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Generative Dynamics</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Inverse Problems</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Physical Constraints</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section research-theme" id="structure-aware-sciml">
    <div class="theme-card">
      <p class="section-eyebrow theme-number">Theme 02</p>
      <DenoiseHeading text="Structure-Aware Scientific Machine Learning" client:visible />
        <p>
          Scientific systems often come with valuable mathematical knowledge, such as governing equations, geometric relationships, and known solution structures. Incorporating this knowledge into machine learning can improve data efficiency, generalization, and computational performance.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am interested in developing models that exploit this structure rather than learning everything from data alone. My work includes robust training methods for physics-informed neural networks and efficient learning of PDE solution operators. In particular, Green's Observation Operators exploit the structure of physical systems to predict responses on lower-dimensional observation domains without repeatedly solving the full underlying PDE.
        </p>
        <p>
          More broadly, I study how analytical modeling and machine learning can complement one another to build efficient, reliable models for scientific applications.
        </p>
      <div class="keyword-row" aria-label="Key ideas for structure-aware scientific machine learning">
        <span class="keyword-pill">Physics-Informed Learning</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">PDEs</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Operator Learning</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Surrogate Modeling</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Scientific Computing</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section research-theme" id="optimization-design">
    <div class="theme-card">
      <p class="section-eyebrow theme-number">Theme 03</p>
      <DenoiseHeading text="Control, Optimization, and Automated Design" client:visible />
        <p>
          Ultimately, I am interested in machine learning methods that go beyond representing physical systems and help us make decisions about them. My earlier work in reinforcement learning explored this perspective in high-performance control, combining performance optimization with constraints derived from expert behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          Building on this background, my longer-term research direction is automated scientific and engineering design. Here, generative models can represent and propose plausible candidates in complex, high-dimensional design spaces, while optimization methods guide exploration towards high-performing solutions.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am particularly interested in closed-loop, multi-fidelity optimization, where learned physical surrogate models, numerical simulations, and real-world experiments provide complementary sources of feedback. An important research challenge is how generative models and adaptive optimization methods can interact to efficiently discover diverse, feasible, and high-performing designs.
        </p>
      <div class="keyword-row" aria-label="Key ideas for optimization and design">
        <span class="keyword-pill">Generative Optimization</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Bayesian Optimization</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Multi-Fidelity Learning</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Reinforcement Learning</span>
        <span class="keyword-pill">Automated Design</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section research-contact" id="contact">
    <DenoiseHeading text="Contact" client:visible />
    <div class="contact-grid">
      <article class="contact-card" id="collaboration">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">Collaboration</p>
        <p>
          If you are working on related topics and think there might be an interesting connection, I would be very happy to hear from you. Feel free to reach out by email for potential collaborations, discussions, or simply to exchange ideas.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button" href="mailto:jan.tauberschmidt@dfki.de?subject=Let%27s%20Collaborate!">Let's collaborate!</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="contact-card" id="supervision">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">Supervision</p>
        <p>
          I am also open to supervising motivated students for thesis projects. If you are interested in working with
          me, please send me an email and briefly describe what you are excited about in particular, what background you
          bring, and which kind of problem you would like to work on. You do not need to have a fully developed project
          proposal, but it is helpful if you already have a concrete direction or research question that genuinely
          interests you.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button" href="mailto:jan.tauberschmidt@dfki.de?subject=Thesis%20Supervision">Register interest for thesis</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-section {
    padding: clamp(1.35rem, 3.8vw, 2.45rem) 0;
  }

  .page-section:first-child {
    padding-top: clamp(1.7rem, 4.2vw, 2.9rem);
  }

  .page-section p {
    text-align: justify;
    text-justify: inter-word;
  }

  .theme-card,
  .contact-card {
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, var(--border));
    border-radius: 1.1rem;
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 4%, white), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.98));
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }

  .research-overview {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .research-overview .section-eyebrow,
  .theme-grid .section-eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }

  .research-thesis {
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .theme-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .theme-link-card {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 10%, var(--border));
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.05rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      border-color 160ms ease,
      transform 160ms ease,
      background 160ms ease;
  }

  .theme-link-card:hover,
  .theme-link-card:focus-visible {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 28%, var(--border));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 3%, white);
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }

  .theme-link-card:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .theme-link-card h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  .theme-link-card p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .research-theme .theme-card :global(h2) {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .theme-number {
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  .keyword-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .keyword-pill {
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 10%, var(--border));
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.32rem 0.72rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .research-contact {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.15rem;
  }

  .contact-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .contact-card {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
  }

  .contact-card .section-eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }

  .contact-card .button-row {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .theme-grid,
    .contact-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
